## Q6 — Elastic String and Energy (10 marks)
**Spec ref**: MC2, MC4, MC5, MC6
### Question
Use g = 9.8 m s⁻².
A light elastic string has natural length 3 m and modulus of elasticity 18 N. One end is attached to a particle of mass 0.25 kg, the other to a fixed point O. The particle is released from rest at point A, which is 4.5 m vertically below O.
**(a)** Calculate the elastic potential energy of the string when the particle is at A. [2 marks]
**(b)** Point B is 3 m vertically below O. Calculate the GPE gained by the particle as it moves from A to B. [2 marks]
**(c)** Find the speed of the particle at B. [3 marks]
**(d)** Point C is 3.6 m vertically below O. Explain, showing calculations, why the speed of the particle is increasing the first time it is at C. [3 marks]
### Solution
**(a)**
Extension at A: x = 4.5 − 3 = 1.5 m
$$\text{EPE} = \frac{\lambda x^2}{2l} = \frac{18 \times 1.5^2}{2 \times 3} = \frac{18 \times 2.25}{6} = \frac{40.5}{6} = 6.75 \text{ J}$$
**(b)**
Distance from A to B = 4.5 − 3 = 1.5 m (particle moves upward)
$$\text{GPE gained} = mgh = 0.25 \times 9.8 \times 1.5 = 3.675 \text{ J}$$
**(c)**
At B, the string is at its natural length, so EPE = 0.
By conservation of energy (EPE at A = KE at B + GPE gained):
$$6.75 = \frac{1}{2}(0.25)v^2 + 3.675$$
$$\frac{1}{2}(0.25)v^2 = 6.75 - 3.675 = 3.075$$
$$0.125v^2 = 3.075$$
$$v^2 = 24.6$$
$$v = \sqrt{24.6} \approx 4.96 \approx 5.0 \text{ m s}^{-1}$$
### Answer (c)
**v = 5.0 m s⁻¹**
**(d)**
At C, the string extension is 3.6 − 3 = 0.6 m.
Tension in string at C:
$$T = \frac{\lambda x}{l} = \frac{18 \times 0.6}{3} = 3.6 \text{ N}$$
Weight of particle:
$$mg = 0.25 \times 9.8 = 2.45 \text{ N}$$
The tension acts upward (toward O) and weight acts downward.
Resultant upward force = T − mg = 3.6 − 2.45 = **1.15 N upward**
Since the particle is moving upward (from A toward B, passing through C on the way) and the resultant force is also upward, the particle is **accelerating in the direction of motion**, so its speed is increasing. □
